(Plan 292: `Append <text var> to <buffer>.` must produce the correct
bytes when the buffer is declared at top level and mutated from
inside a function (a "global" buffer, resolved through its global
mirror in codegen) - not just when both live in the same top-level
scope (the "local" case tests/217 and tests/218 already cover). Plan
290 widened Append to accept a text-variable source; this uncovered a
register clobber specific to the global-buffer codegen fallback:
`emit_load_named_var_addr` overwrote rax - which still held the
source value from `generate_expr` - before that value was consumed,
so the runtime ended up appending a fragment of the destination
buffer's own header bytes onto itself instead of the intended text.)

a buffer called g is 64 bytes in size.
a text called payload is "PAYLOAD".

To mutate.
  Append payload to g.

mutate.
Print g.

(Control: the literal-source case in the exact same
function-mutates-global-buffer shape already worked before this fix
and must not regress)
a buffer called g2 is 64 bytes in size.

To mutate_literal.
  Append "PAYLOAD" to g2.

mutate_literal.
Print g2.

(Sibling path: the same text-variable source appended to a buffer
that stays local to the current scope - the dst_local path this fix
must not touch - printed here for a direct side-by-side comparison
against the global case above)
a buffer called local_buf is 64 bytes in size.
a text called payload2 is "PAYLOAD".
Append payload2 to local_buf.
Print local_buf.

(Two separate appends into two different global buffers from two
different function calls in sequence, to confirm the fix's push/pop
pair around the destination-address load stays balanced across
repeated invocations rather than drifting the stack)
mutate.
Print g.
